<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Altenburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Altenburg is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>. An imperial castle and town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pleissenland">Pleissenland</a>, the imperial territory later absorbed by the Wettins. From 1603 it was the residence of the dukes of Saxe-Altenburg, and it is known as the place where the card game <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skat">Skat</a> was developed in the early 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/altenburg.jpg" alt="Altenburg" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Friese · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altenburg_Frauenfels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
